  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1249 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nThursday Night-Sunday...A subtle trough in the subtropical jet \nis forecasted to move into Southern Plains Thursday night into \nFriday, causing pressure falls along the Front Range in \nColorado. As zonal flow aloft turns southwesterly, the cold \nfront from Thursday will transition into a warm front and move \nnorthward. Scattered showers and storms (40-80% coverage) are \nlikely to develop along this boundary Friday into Saturday \nmorning. By Saturday, the trough reaches Iowa and northern \nMissouri, keeping PoPs (40-60%) on the board heading into \nSunday. Rainfall totals of 0.1 to 0.5 inches are probable \n(70-90% chance) areawide through Sunday.\n\nEarly Next Week...Longwave troughing over the western CONUS is \nexpected to become in phase with the subtropical jet and eject \neastward into the Rockies. Surface cyclogenesis is likely across\nthe Central Plains with a warm front extending eastward into \nthe Mid-Mississippi River Valley. Beyond this point, there is \ngreat uncertainty among the ensemble guidance in the development\nof this mid-latitude cyclone and its path, but confidence is \nhigh (70-90% chance) on a wet and breezy pattern early next \nweek.",
